hink that depends on the  circumstances and if you had a  disagreement with the president how  would you raise it again depends on the  circumstances I think it starts by  having a direct conversation it depends  on what the issue is you're here you  live in Washington so you also know that  much of this town is also talking about  Bob Woodward's book called  year and in that book he describes an  administration which you are part of but  he's specifically describing the White  House as having a nervous breakdown of  executive power have you experienced any  of that I have not seen a nervous  breakdown of executive power I have also  not read mr. Woodward's book and I'm not  sure whether I'll ever find time how  would you describe your relationship  with President Trump my focus is on  having a very professional relationship  with the president I think that's what  I've been trying to do since day one and  that's true today just as much as it was  on day one do you meet with him  one-on-one  no you usually meet with the Attorney  General the Deputy Attorney General well  I certainly had plenty of meetings with  the president I just haven't met with  him one-on-one I usually meet with him  with a small group of other people and  it depends on what the issue is he  tweets a lot about cabinet members and  top members of his administration but  you specifically have never been the  subject of one of his tweets well I'm  not much of a Twitter guy as people will  tell you and that's not meant to  criticise Twitter at social media  commentary has its place I just find  that I've got enough to do all day long  running the FBI that without getting too  hung up and worrying about what's on  Twitter mm-hmm let me turn now to Russia  and its malign activities when Vladimir  Putin said that Russia has never  interfered and is not going to interfere  in American affairs including the  election process was he lying I'm not  gonna accuse somebody of lying I'll just  say that that doesn't jibe with our read  of the evidence and we're pretty  confident in our read what is Russia  doing to disrupt the midterms that are  now just about 60 days away so what  we're seeing now is a continuation of  the what we call it the malign foreign  influence efforts what they do is so  both inaccurate information  disinformation it's the kind of  information warfare and then propaganda  exaggerated hashed truths distortions  and they do both by in clandestine and  covert means they fan it out through  social media and try to pit Americans  against each other and they took  quickly will seize upon you know some  social issue and try to gin up both  sides so that we turn on each other  and so we've known that for several  years well I think what has been  happening for a number of years but it  continues to happen relentlessly to this  day and the the use of social media is a  bullhorn that allows that kind of  information warfare to be inflicted much  more effectively so that's why it's  something that remains a concern and  could influence individual voters if  we're not as Americans vigilant about  who's saying what and where their  information is coming from as director  of the FBI I know you created this  foreign influence task force to try and  funnel some of this actionable  intelligence on so some changes could be  made how has that changed things how has  that made the election process safer  well the foreign influence task force is  something that we created last year to  bring together the different disciplines  of the FBI which is designed to do a  number of things one is to make sure  that we ensure that we have the right  kinds of investigations throughout all  of our field offices whether it's a  counterintelligence investigation a  cyber attack a criminal investigation  all of which are designed to deal with  that we're also doing a better job of  communicating with state local election  officials about information that they  should have working close to the  Department of Homeland Security and then  of course we're also working very  closely with the private sector  especially the technology sector in  order to help them be more effective and  aggressive in protecting their own  platforms from abuse in fact just in  april facebook announced that it had  dismantled or taken down hundreds of  accounts that were tied to the internet  research agency which is essentially a  Russian troll farm that is pushing out a  lot of this information warfare when  Facebook did that was that based on the  actionable intelligence from the FBI  well I can't discuss in this kind of  setting specific intelligence specific  investigations but what I can tell you  is that we have a much much closer  working relationship  if the prominent companies in Silicon  Valley where we share information with  them they can take certain steps and  then they share information with us and  we get sort of a back and forth that's  very much for the best benefit of the  American people why are we letting this  happen  why are we letting Russian do this we  have the greatest law enforcement agency  in the world the FBI we have the most  powerful tech companies in the world why  are we allowing Russia to do this I  don't view us as allowing them to do  anything  I think we're countering it we are  working hard to counter it more more  effectively all fighting back well in  some cases we have law enforcement  investigations that lead to charges in  some cases we have steps that the  technology companies can take themselves  and in some cases were raising awareness  because the best defense against  disinformation and propaganda is  accurate information and the more the  American people are careful about what  they're hearing and who they're hearing  it from the more protected we are  there's a top priority for you it  absolutely has to be our democracy is  one of the things that we're most proud  of in this country and we rightly so is  it a top priority for this White House I  think it is I think the the fact that  you had the FBI director the Director of  National Intelligence the Secretary of  Homeland Security the head of NSA all  together along with the National  Security Advisor  holding a conference together as a  reflection of how seriously we all take  this subject that was just once in  August well I don't want to measure our  efforts by by a press conference but I  think that's a reflection of what we're  doing we have lots and lots of meetings  behind the scene and more importantly at  the levels below us we have lots of  people working very hard to protect  American clarify that then because while  the FBI has been working and other  agencies have been working to counter  this election meddling there are some  who have said there is no one driving  this bus there's no one from the White  House who's in charge making sure that  the communication is going on when was  the last time that there was a White  House meeting on this issue I mean we've  had a number of meetings I don't know  that I could think through on my  calendar when the last one was but we've  had quite a few over the last several  months  so not a concern for you the number of  meetings well that there's cordon  within the government on this issue that  it's a top issue I will say I've heard  that complaint I think it's a red  herring to be honest what I find when I  talked to our folks when I talked to my  counterparts at Homeland Security or NSA  or odni the Director of National  Intelligence or elsewhere in the  community the CIA etc everyone's focused  on and everyone knows what their jobs  are everyone's working hard so come  November when the tally is announced of  the votes can Americans be confident  that it was a fair election I think  Americans can have confidence in our  election system we talked about the  intelligence assessment that Russia is  trying to weaken and divide the United  States there was a Pew poll out recently  about the reputation of the FBI and it  showed a 28-point  partisan gap in terms of how the FBI is  viewed 77% of Democrats were positively  supportive of the FBI forty-nine percent  of Republicans amongst Republicans  that's fallen 16 percent in just the  last year why do you think there are  some questions about the reputation of  the FBI well I think sometimes watching  the news and certainly trolling the  Internet you would think that the FBI  has only had two investigations over the  last three years but I will tell you  that when you get out into real America  people know differently I've been to 43  already of our 56 field offices and  we'll get to all 56 in short order  I've met with something like 2,500 of  our state local and foreign partners I  met with prosecutors I've met with  victims I've met with judges and in  office after office what I encounter  from the people who actually experience  the FBI through our work is a very  different story and we're not driven by  poll numbers there's one thing the FBI  is not about it's poll numbers we're  about the work and about the people we  work for and that's the American people  that's the victims that we're trying to  protect from whether it's kidnappings or  terrorist attacks or gang violence  that's what we're about  do you feel like you have support from  the White House for the FBI's mission I  do I think we're doing what we need to  do I don't really get too hung up on and  I don't think our folks get too hung up  on what's on social media to be honest  we're much more driven by what to our  partners think of us what are the  prosecutors think what are the judges  think what are the juries think what are  the victims and their families think  what of all our federal state local  foreign intelligence community partners  think do they know they can count on us  and over and over and over again the  feedback I get from those people who  know what they're talking about  is uniformly positive and appreciative  directory I know you have been asked  this many times but I do want to ask it  of you because the president has issued  repeated attacks on the FBI on the  integrity of its work on the people who  work here at the FBI in fact he said  that the reputation of the FBI is in  tatters those attacks I have to have  taken their toll I'll tell you what I  see I see 37,000 men and women who get  up every day trying to keep 325 million  American people safe I see people who  work their tails off in that effort who  are people of character of courage of  professionalism and diligence and we do  thousands and thousands of  investigations every year I think about  the agents that I swear in at Quantico  several times a year who compete like  heck to be accepted into the ranks of  the FBI I think about the Chicago agent  the SWAT agent that I thought about the  other day who got shot at point-blank  range by an ak-47 lost the use of his  dominant hand retrained himself to shoot  with his other hand Rhee qualified for  SWAT and then not only said about going  back to work in the mission but to being  a good mentor to an ATF colleague who  had also been shot I think about the  woman in Miami I met who 12 stitches in  her face injured back at it the next day  I think about the guy in Portland that I  met just recently one of our  Tech's who got peppered with shrapnel  from a booby trap that he encountered  when clearing a house back at work the  next day I could give you an example  after example that's the FBI that I see  that's the real FBI and that's what  we're about I mean you're the leader of  an incredible organization the FBI and  all of us are members of different  organizations and when sometimes someone  insults our organization we tell them to  cut it out have you told the President  to cut it out I'm not gonna comment on  my discussions with the president I will  say we're big boys and girls we're used  to criticism there's a lot of opinions  out there by a lot of people and a lot  of settings we're focused on the work  and the people we do the work for you've  addressed this issue but the scathing IG  report that followed the last election  specifically into Hillary Clinton's  emails did that hurt the FBI's  credibility well I think the Inspector  General's report speaks for itself in  many ways and it's 500 something pages  long I would say that we ran it picked  by both sides well we're an organization  that's a hundred and ten years old that  and we're an organization 37,000 people  and I would say that just like any  organization we've made our share of  mistakes over the years what defines us  ultimately is whether we learn lessons  from those mistakes and we have done  that over and over again including in  this instance and come out better and  stronger from it and that's what's going  to happen here let me ask you about the  Catholic Church it has been in the news  as of late and there are now attorney  generals in several states that have  launched investigations into the  Catholic Church and abuse by priests at  what point does this rise to the level  of a national investigation where the  FBI would get involved I'm not going to  be discussing whether we're going to be  conducting a particular investigation or  not I would tell you that in my view  there is no set of victims in this  country that are more worthy and more in  need of our protection than our kids and  I think it's a point of pride for  everybody who works in the FBI that we  fight for those who can't fight for  themselves  and I will also say that when we have  the facts and we have the law and we  have the evidence to support an  investigation there is no institution  that were afraid to investigate period  now we've done lots and lots of work to  keep kids in this country safe from lots  of different kinds of predators just in  the course of this year alone we've  rescued something like thirteen hundred  thirteen fifty almost fourteen hundred  kids  rescued them how so these would be kids  who have been kidnapped or abducted or  exploited in some way it's something we  take very personally the FBI yeah the  FBI is on the front lines of taking care  of children rescuing them amongst all  the other things that they do right and  we view our job as both to get the bad  guy and to get the child back the issue  of attacks on journalists the president  has frequently called the press the  enemy of the American people the FBI  just recently foiled a plot against the  Boston Globe where the man had  threatened to essentially shoot up the  newsroom and he used the phrase enemy of  the people is the FBI investigating  threats to journalists we do have a  number of investigations involving  threats to journalists just in the past  the course of this year I would say  we've got maybe a half dozen or so  investigations of threats to journalists  in particular but that's not counting  all the ones that we work on with our  state local law enforcement partners as  well certainly for example we have the  active shooter situation in Annapolis  with the paper there we work closely  with our state and local counterparts to  try to be of assistance there we have  our evidence recovery teams of earnest  response teams we have our lab we have  our SWAT teams so we do a lot of things  to deploy but in my view threats of  violence much less actual violence to  the news media is something that we  cannot and will not tolerate it  let's turn to China and the efforts of  China here in the United States would  you say that China has been more  successful than Russia in terms of  infiltrating American intelligence well  I do think that China that there's no  country that presents a more broad and  more comprehensive threat to our ideas  to our innovation to our economic  security in many ways to so much of our  daily lives than China does and that  takes a lot of different forms and it is  the FBI's top counterintelligence  priority and it needs to be so our men  and women are grinding away on this  issue day in and day out your top  counterintelligence party is China it's  not Russia we spent a lot of time  working on Russia - but if I look at our  counterintelligence mission overall  China is our top priority in that space  we've had cases involving everything  from turbine technology in places like  upstate New York to corn seed  development in Iowa what do you mean  corn see they're trying to steal our  corn seed well of course we have I think  America's agriculture is the envy of the  world and we're very proud of it and we  should be and whenever we're the best at  something somebody else is chasing us  it's something that I think most  Americans don't understand what are they  trying to steal what do they want  they're trying to steal our trade  secrets our ideas our innovation our  assets if you will our ideas it sounds  like we're under attack well I don't  know that I would use that word but I  would say that we are in a situation  where there is widespread intellectual  property theft economic espionage cyber  intrusions and those are things that we  are going to fight back against the US  Trade Representative put a figure on the  theft of intellectual property and it's  up to six hundred billion dollars  annually it's enormous  the thing that people need to  is that this has an impact on everyday  people it has an impact on American  businesses it has an impact on American  jobs it has an impact on American  consumers how so well China's goal is to  take what it can and become essentially  self-sufficient and put American  businesses out of business  well to replace America as the world's  economic superpower I think that's their  goal and they're pretty open about I  will stack our innovation our system our  people up against any but it's a threat  that's real and it's something that we  need to take very seriously and it  involves not just the Chinese government  it involves Chinese businesses both  state-owned businesses as well as  Chinese ostensibly private businesses  that advance the Chinese government's  agenda or in some cases are even  directed by the Chinese government it  involves not just traditional spies you  know the old-school spy versus spy kind  of thing but also other types of people  who are sent here by China either  pressured or incentivized to steal  information and bring it back to some  arm of the Chinese government I mean the  FBI has indicted professors in the past  what threat did they pose Chinese  professors well I have to speak can't  speak to any specific case but certainly  we have situations where we have  research scientists who come from China  who work in very sensitive fields of  study that are very relevant to our  national security that involve National  Defense Information there's a very close  an appropriate partnership between the  scientific community and our defense  infrastructure in this country and some  situations there are academics who take  that information and bring it back to  the Chinese but I think as you were just  saying they're not traditional Chinese  spies they're in academia they're in all  kinds of different fields that are  carrying out this type of economic  espionage that's right these are people  who are in some cases research  scientists in some cases businesspeople  in some cases other kinds of research  some cases they could be students more  likely graduate students and  undergraduate students in some cases  they could be students again not for a  minute do I want to suggest that all  Chinese visitors are somehow up to no  good  not at all but there are lots of them  and we have lots of investigations  ongoing as we just discussed how many  arrests is the FBI made of Chinese spies  I don't have that number handy billa  Vanina who's the country's top  counterintelligence official he told CBS  News that China was dedicating ungodly  resources to espionage and that it was  more aggressive than ever he said that  the FBI had arrested more than two dozen  Chinese spies well depending on the time  period I have I'm quite confident that  would be an accurate number it just  depends on what time period we're  certainly I think we probably arrested a  lot more than that depending how far  back in time you go since 2016 5 current  or former employees some of whom worked  here the FBI have been charged with  espionage so what is the FBI doing to  make sure you don't have spies here well  what you're describing there is what we  call the insider threat and that's  something that we and the rest of our  partners in the intelligence community  are very focused on which is situations  where somebody already working in the  intelligence community is recruited or  co-opted by a foreign intelligence  service in this case the Chinese and one  of our core missions going back 110  years is to protect America from that  threat and we investigate it very very  aggressively and carefully and we intend  to continue doing that given the scope  of that Chinese espionage and efforts to  steal our American technology that could  be up to six hundred billion dollars  annually what's the FBI doing to  counteract that how do you do it how do  you prevent the stealing of wind turbine  technology corn seed technology  everything that's out there that they're  trying to steal well it starts with  vigilance and so even though we are  devoting lots of resources  of agents analysts and professional  staff to lots of investigations in this  space in order to be able to get the  kind of coverage that we're talking  about here we need to make sure that  we're partnering more and more closely  with American business with the private  sector with American universities and  we're doing a lot these days  all over the country to meet with and  engage and brief those institutions  whether their companies or research  institutions and to try to encourage  them what to be on the lookout for and  in many cases they can take steps to  protect themselves which helps us all in  this common fight it is a many ways that  kind of a whole of American society  defense here and just to drill down on  this to give you know our viewers just  kind of a sense of what's going on this  was public than in August a General  Electric engineer was arrested for  stealing plans for power turbines how  did the FBI find out about that well I  want to be a little bit careful what I  say about a specific investigation other  than to say that we partner very closely  with American businesses and a company  like that is a classic example of a  situation where they have certain  expertise access infrastructure we have  certain expertise agents and might and  working together we can come up with a  solution so more broadly speaking since  you've been FBI director are some of the  great American companies coming to you  and saying we think the Chinese are  trying to steal some of our best  technology we one of the things that's  been most gratifying to me in my first  year on the job is how committed and  enthusiastic a lot of American  businesses are to working with us in  this common fight and we have found  situations where they've come to us now  that they know better what to be on the  lookout for and we encourage them to do  that it's the whole see something say  something in a very different context  and we usually use that expression and  then we often will ourselves reach out  and tell them something we're seeing and  sometimes that then prompts a very  productive conversation it's not only  these individuals who are engaged in  spying its companies you've raised  concerns about the Chinese telecom  Giants  Huawei and ZTE why are they national  security risks well anytime you start  talking about foreign companies that are  beholding to foreign governments that  don't share our values and our  dedication to the rule of law enables  them to conduct economic espionage it  enables them to conduct different kinds  of cyberattacks it enables them to steal  information in a variety of ways the  president intervened a few months ago to  save ZTE from some of these crippling  financial penalties that Congress wanted  was the president wrong I continue to be  very concerned and I think the  intelligence community continues to be  very concerned about the threat to our  telecommunications infrastructure  presented by some of the kinds of  companies that are beholden to foreign  governments that don't share our values  and the idea of letting the Fox in the  henhouse is something that I think  people need to be really really careful  about before we find out that we're  going to regret it when you talk about  your tenure as FBI director you've been  in office just over a year now has been  a tough year how would you describe it  you know it's had its challenges of  course but I knew that coming in I think  that this is an organization that has  some of the greatest people in the world  and has one of the most important  missions in the world and I think it's a  privilege to be able to work with these  people and as I make my way around the  country then to 43 of our 56 field  offices and met with them I've met with  every headquarters division I've met  with a whole bunch of our offices  overseas over and over and over again  I meet unbelievably inspiring  hard-working dedicated people who come  from all walks of life who are unified  by one thing which is to protect the  American people and uphold the  Constitution and I feel very lucky to be  a part of it much less as the director  mm-hmm your predecessor was fired James  Cohen was fired by the president United  States did you want this job I was not  seeking this job I was in private  practice I thought my law on force  the days were behind me and I was doing  quite well in private practice but when  the opportunity arose and I started  talking with some of my friends from the  FBI who had been agents that I worked  with when I was a line prosecutor in  Atlanta who were agents who worked at  headquarters when I was here on and  after 9/11 the thought of coming back  into this space was exciting to me and I  believe so strongly in their mission and  in their character that the opportunity  to do it was something that I didn't  think I could pass up you've been called  a steady hand described as understated  and that you give everybody their day in  court what is it that you want the  American people to know about you that  they may not know my mantra my motto  that I've expressed over and over again  to our folks is keep calm and tackle  hard and that basically boils down to  there's nobody yelling or screaming or  anything else that's gonna get the FBI  off track as long as I have anything to  say about it we're gonna focus on the  work on the mission which is protecting  the American people and upholding the  Constitution everything else is noise so  in the midst of a lot of partisan  bickering and back-and-forth you're what  focused on the mission that sounds like  I think what the American people should  expect deserve from the FBI and from the  FBI director is straight shooter and  that's what they're gonna get thank you  thank you

